&lt;p>On top of that, the patterns and the session loop emit:&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Integration guide</title><link>/integration/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/integration/</guid><description>&lt;p>The bundled question service is a placeholder. It hosts the intake
catalog and an answer validator so the demo runs out of the box, but in
your build it will be replaced by whatever already owns your business
data: a CRM, a policy admin system, an EHR, an internal microservice,
or a stack of them. This page walks you through the two seams the
sample is designed around.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="seam-1-replace-the-question-service">Seam 1: replace the question service&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>The backend talks to the question service through a single thin module:
three async functions, one HTTP base URL.&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Origins &amp; lineage</title><link>/origins/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/origins/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="the-upstream-reference">The upstream reference&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>The patterns are not invented here. They come straight from
&lt;a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-realtime-agents">&lt;code>openai/openai-realtime-agents&lt;/code>&lt;/a>,
a Next.js / TypeScript demo from OpenAI that introduced both:&lt;/p>
&lt;ul>
&lt;li>Chat-Supervisor. A low-latency realtime &amp;ldquo;chat&amp;rdquo; agent paired with a
smarter text-only supervisor (&lt;code>gpt-4.1&lt;/code>) that handles tool calls and
high-stakes reasoning.&lt;/li>
&lt;li>Sequential Handoff. A graph of specialist agents that hand the caller
off to one another via tool calls, all on a single realtime session.
Inspired by &lt;a href="https://github.com/openai/swarm">OpenAI Swarm&lt;/a>.&lt;/li>
&lt;/ul>
&lt;p>If you have not read the upstream README, do that first. The why and the
benefits of each pattern are explained there in detail. This sample
assumes you understand them and shows how to build them on Azure.&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Pattern 1 — Chat-Supervisor</title><link>/chat-supervisor/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/chat-supervisor/</guid><description>&lt;p>Chat-Supervisor is the simpler of the two patterns. It keeps the voice
contract small (one agent, one persona, one system prompt) and shoves
all the &amp;ldquo;should I accept this answer?&amp;rdquo; and &amp;ldquo;is this claim complete?&amp;rdquo;
decisions out to a text model that the user never hears.&lt;/p>
&lt;figure class="shot">
 &lt;a href="/img/03-chat-supervisor-conversation.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
 &lt;img src="/img/03-chat-supervisor-conversation.png" alt="Chat-Supervisor mid-conversation: Aria has greeted the caller, asked about the incident date, and is asking for the location." loading="lazy" />
 &lt;/a>
 &lt;figcaption class="shot__cap">Aria, the realtime voice agent, drives the entire conversation.&lt;/figcaption>
&lt;/figure>

&lt;h2 id="the-voice-agent">The voice agent&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>There is exactly one prompt. It tells Aria how to behave, when to call
tools, and (this part matters) that she must never invent claim numbers
or policy details on her own.&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Pattern 2 — Sequential Handoff</title><link>/sequential-handoff/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/sequential-handoff/</guid><description>&lt;p>Sequential Handoff shows a workflow where the caller is explicitly
moved between specialists (&lt;code>greeter&lt;/code>, &lt;code>top_level_qa&lt;/code>, &lt;code>auto_claims&lt;/code>,
&lt;code>property_claims&lt;/code>, &lt;code>health_claims&lt;/code>, and &lt;code>summary&lt;/code>), but where each
&amp;ldquo;agent&amp;rdquo; is just a different prompt and tool list applied to the same
Voice Live API session.&lt;/p>
&lt;figure class="shot">
 &lt;a href="/img/11-sequential-handoff-initial.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
 &lt;img src="/img/11-sequential-handoff-initial.png" alt="Sequential handoff initial state. Greeter is the active agent and the agent progress list shows all six agents." loading="lazy" />
 &lt;/a>
 &lt;figcaption class="shot__cap">Every call starts with the greeter. The Agent Progress list previews the chain to come.&lt;/figcaption>
&lt;/figure>

&lt;h2 id="agents-are-just-configs">Agents are just configs&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>An agent is a small dataclass with three things: a name, a system prompt,
and a list of allowed handoff targets. Specialists also carry the business
tools (&lt;code>validate_answer&lt;/code>, &lt;code>get_next_questions&lt;/code>, &lt;code>submit_claim_data&lt;/code>).&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Session lifecycle</title><link>/session-lifecycle/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/session-lifecycle/</guid><description>&lt;p>A Voice Live API session is a single bidirectional WebSocket between the
backend and Azure. It is opened once per call, configured once with a
&lt;code>session.update&lt;/code>, and then carries audio in both directions plus a stream
of event messages until the user hangs up.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>The sample uses the official &lt;code>azure.ai.voicelive.aio&lt;/code> SDK. Opening a
session is one async context manager.&lt;/p>
&lt;figure class="coderef">
 &lt;figcaption class="coderef__cap">
