&lt;p>Once &lt;code>connect()&lt;/code> returns, two coroutines run concurrently for the
lifetime of the call. &lt;code>_read_from_browser&lt;/code> pulls inbound audio bytes
or control JSON off the thread-safe queue and forwards them to Azure.
&lt;code>_read_from_azure&lt;/code> consumes events from Azure, maps them to client event
types, and pushes them onto the outbound queue.&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Validation flow</title><link>/validation/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/validation/</guid><description>&lt;p>The validation flow is the most concrete demo of why this sample uses
two LLMs. The realtime model is great at speaking, but it should not be
the sole judge of &amp;ldquo;is this answer specific enough to record?&amp;rdquo;. That
decision belongs to a deterministic validator, with an LLM judge as a
tiebreaker.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="the-trigger-a-vague-answer">The trigger: a vague answer&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>Open the app in &lt;strong>Text Mode&lt;/strong>, start a Chat-Supervisor session, and
answer the incident date with something like &lt;em>&amp;ldquo;yesterday around 3pm&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em>.
The question service rejects it immediately.&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>